hurricanes floods wildfires 2021 brought  more than its fair share of natural  disasters and as well as claiming around  10 000 lives they also took a major  financial toll german reinsurer munich  ray says it was one of the most  expensive years on record with the  global damages estimated at around 280  billion dollars only 120 billion dollars  of that was insured hardest hit was the  united states which accounted for 150  billion dollars of the damages it was  struck by hurricane ida disastrous  tornadoes an extremely cold winter in  the south and wildfires in the west  meanwhile let's look at europe floods  did the most damage there 54 billion  dollars worth in total last year's  flooding in western germany was the  country's worst and most costly natural  disaster on record meanwhile let's look  at asia the asia-pacific region saw  damages of 48 billion dollars but a mere  eight billion of that was actually  covered by insurance so what made 2021 a  remarkable year for natural disasters  well that's a question i put to chief  climate and geoscientists at munich re  and ralph  well 2021 proved to be the second  costliest year ever for the insurance  industry with respect to losses from  natural catastrophes  the total bill came at 120 billion us  dollars  uh the record before was set in 2017  with 146 billion us dollars so in 2021  we had for instance a major hurricane  hurricane ida in the united states of  america where this event alone did cost  the insurance industry some 36 billion  us dollars  and from a european perspective we had  this major flash flooding event in july  in germany and neighboring countries  holland and belgium and this event  resulted in insured losses of 13 billion  us dollars so there were a couple of  major events uh  contributing to this second highest  record number for the insurance industry  in 2021  are we talking about an issue of climate  change here if it would appear that the  number of natural disasters is trending  upwards  uh well that's indeed the case in some  regions and with some perils  what we cannot prove is with respect to  the data we are analyzing at munich is  that worldwide losses from natural  disasters are increasing it's mainly  events from flooding and so called  convective storms and there are two main  drivers the one is simply the  socio-economic changes so increase in  population and wealth and with  increasing wealth we obviously uh have  to expect higher losses as well  but the other factor is not really  man-made in a strict sense yes it is  climate change contributing uh from the  emissions of carbon dioxide  and other greenhouse gases and here we  see changing weather patterns  especially increasing probabilities and  intensities of severe weather events and  the  flash floods which we have seen in  germany and  neighboring countries in 2021 is a good  example  science with attribution studies clearly  tells us that the probability of such  events has increased over time driven by  climate change global warming higher  temperatures and higher moisture content  in the atmosphere right so how do people  and businesses react to that increased  probability is it a case of just  covering yourself with insurance  well the the first and most important  step is to get informed informed about  your local and regional exposure to  different types of natural perils  that can be river flooding it can be  flash floods  it can be tropical cycle it really  depends on where you live and only if  you inform yourself and that's  individuals it's homeowners businesses  but also risk manager in in the public  sector so only if you know about your  local risk situation you can take  actions an action can be  mitigation measures or lot loss  prevention measures  anything from drainage systems water  tight  windows for instance hardening of roofs  when it comes to damages from high wind  speeds but the ultimate solution also  can be to buy more insurance coverage  for your property  okay so work out what the risks are and  prepare yourself for them and rauk from  munich thank you so much for joining us  on tw business
 

